# Backend hosts for the ZBROwner app.
#
# Expo inlines EXPO_PUBLIC_* variables into the JS bundle at BUILD time — they
# are not read at runtime. So the values present when you bundle are the values
# shipped to the vendor's phone. Changing them later requires a rebuild.
#
# Which file is loaded (later overrides earlier):
#   .env              always — not used by this project
#   .env.development  NODE_ENV=development — COMMITTED, points dev at production
#   .env.production   NODE_ENV=production  — COMMITTED, release builds
#   .env.local        always, overrides everything, GITIGNORED — machine-specific
#
# You do NOT need to copy this file. Both .env.development and .env.production
# are committed and already point at https://zbrr.uz, so a fresh clone works
# against production in every mode.
#
# To develop against a LOCAL backend, create `.env.local` — it is gitignored and
# overrides the committed defaults:
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.50:8080
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_BASE_URL=ws://192.168.1.50:8080
#
# Restart with `npx expo start --clear` after changing any env file — these
# values are inlined at bundle time and Metro caches them.
#
# Use your machine's LAN IP, never localhost — a phone resolves localhost to
# itself, not to your computer.
#
# RELEASE builds MUST use https:// and wss:// — iOS ATS and the Android
# cleartext policy block plain http/ws, so a release build pointed at http://
# simply cannot reach the backend. Put those in `.env.production`.
#
# Verify what actually got baked in before uploading:  npm run check:release

EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
EXPO_PUBLIC_WS_BASE_URL=ws://localhost:8080
